  "body": "Although #1903 is working on aligning the \"user activation\" hook with browsers, we also need to align browsers and specs on where that hook is used. So far I know of the following in specs:\r\n\r\n- HTML\r\n  - [allow-top-navigation-by-user-activation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#allowed-to-navigate)\r\n  - [handoff to external software when clicking links](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#hand-off-to-external-software) (a \"should\")\r\n  - [allowed to play media](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#allowed-to-play) (as a potential implementation, not even a should)\r\n  - [file upload](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#file-upload-state-(type=file))\r\n  - [popup blocker](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-rules-for-choosing-a-browsing-context-given-a-browsing-context-name)\r\n- [Fullscreen](https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#allowed-to-request-fullscreen)\r\n\r\nIt sounds like [Chrome wants to introduce a new use](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/forum/#!topic/blink-dev/kPli8ZCUeok) for navigation, which \"consumes\" the user activation but does not require one. If that ships successfully, and #1903 goes through successfully so that the \"consumes\" concept becomes a cross-browser interoperable idea, then we can try to spec Chrome's behavior there too.",
